  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The distance $d$ is directly proportional to the square of the time $t$, so we have\n\n$$\nd=k{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $d=12\\,\\text{m}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =k\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =k\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{12}{4} & =k \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of variation is $k=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
